{
  "state": {
    "case_id": "45",
    "case_summary": "In March 2002, a 27-year-old male experienced a sudden loss of central vision in the right eye, followed by a similar loss in the left eye within 18 days. At the onset, he was an occasional light drinker and smoked moderately (15 cigarettes/day for 5 years; cotinine and trans-3′-hydroxycotinine levels were not measured). He had chronic headaches that responded to common painkillers, which worsened after starting work in a glue factory in 1998. The headaches intensified significantly when he was transferred to the glue preparation department, where he worked for 6 months before the vision loss. Fundus examination revealed micro-angiopathy and pseudoedema of the optic disc in both eyes. A corticosteroid treatment cycle was ineffective. One month later, visual acuity was 1/100 in the right eye and 7/10 in the left eye. In June 2002, bilateral paleness of the optic discs was observed, and the patient could not count fingers with either eye. He also reported disturbing photopsias. Treatment with idebenone (270 mg/day) and brimonidine (2 drops, 3 times/day) was started.\n\nMitochondrial (mt) DNA testing on a blood sample was positive for homoplasmic 11778G → A/ND4 mutation. Haplogroup definition showed that this patient’s mtDNA belonged to haplogroup H (DdeI 10394-, AluI 7025-, MseI 14766-).\n\nWhat is the most likely diagnosis?\n",
